            <ArticleTitle>Paediatrics: surgical strategy and quality of life in craniopharyngioma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>447-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/nrendo.2013.125</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Management of childhood craniopharyngioma remains controversial. Aggressive primary surgery is associated with an increased risk of severe obesity, but hypothalamus-sparing surgery might increase the need for reoperation and/or irradiation. Nevertheless, hypothalamus-sparing strategies decreased occurrence of obesity—a risk factor for impaired quality of life—without increasing recurrence rates in a new study.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
